---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `Somebody told me there is more than one way to sort a list. Show
me a different one from the one you already showed me, and tell
me honestly which is better.`;

const SampleOutput = `After pass 1: 17,42,8,55,31,93
After pass 2: 17,8,42,31,55,93
After pass 3: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 4: 8,17,31,42,55,93
Comparisons: 20 (selection sort used 15)

already sorted -> comparisons: 5, passes: 1`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-6" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 6
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Bubble Sort</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code code={Brief} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What they asked for" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-6">Set 6</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Bubble sort compares <b>neighbors</b> and swaps them if they are out of order. Repeat until a full pass makes no swaps at all.</li>
            <li>That last clause is the interesting part. Selection sort could not tell it was finished; this one can.</li>
            <li>Print the list after every pass and count the comparisons.</li>
            <li><b>Then run it on a list that is already sorted</b>, and compare that count against the scrambled one. Then compare both against selection sort's 15.</li>
            <li>Answer the client's question. Give a real answer, not &quot;it depends&quot;.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code code={SampleOutput} frame="terminal" title="Sample Output" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Read those two numbers together.</b> Bubble sort did more work
            than selection sort on the scrambled list and a third of the work on
            the sorted one. Neither algorithm wins outright, and saying which
            one you would ship is the challenge.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-bubble-sort</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
